# The dated Debian archive every hosted guest job installs from, and the date
# of the container image it installs into. `sid` is a rolling release and its
# QEMU is the instrument this project's numbers are taken on: on 2026-08-31 it
# moved 11.1.0 to 11.1.1 mid-afternoon and every guest job refused at
# `instrument.sh`, correctly, because a number taken on one QEMU is not a
# number about the other. Installing from a snapshot makes the instrument a
# decision rather than a delivery date.
#
# Half a pin is not a pin. Dating the packages and leaving `image: debian:sid`
# rolling put every job in an image carrying packages *newer* than this
# archive, and apt will not select the downgrade back to it — `gcc-16-base`,
# run 35572642348, every guest shard of every pull request. So the image is
# dated too: every `image:` that reaches this archive names
# `debian:sid-<this date>` and pins its digest. The dated Debian image is
# debuerreotype's build of this archive at this exact timestamp —
# `sid-20260824` declares `created: 2026-08-24T00:00:00Z` and carries this
# archive's `gcc-16-base 16.2.0-1` — which is what lets the two be one date.
#
# The first run after this date moves pays a cold archive and nothing else:
# `deps` measured 295-428 s across eight shards on the pair's first run
# (35576936014) and 52-66 s on the next one (35577703745), which is the 52-59 s
# that step has always been priced at. A slow `deps` on the day the date moves
# is not the transport and not the image.
#
# This date and `.github/qemu-version` are one fact in two files: the date is
# one on which `sid` carried that QEMU. `qemu-system-x86 1:11.1.0+ds-2` entered
# the archive at 20260818T142548Z and `1:11.1.1+ds-1` replaced it at
# 20260831T145707Z, so a date between those two declares 11.1.0. Moving one of
# the three alone is a mistake, and `src/ci.rs` holds every workflow's copy of
# this date — in its archive URL and in its image tag — against this file.
20260824T000000Z
